    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the build succeeds (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`), including the new structured-address test.\n\n## What I changed\n\nFive files, each touched with a small, self-contained edit that keeps the changed methods simple and out of the large `Owner` class's hot paths:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added optional `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` string properties to `OwnerFields`, and dropped `address` from the `required` list so a structured-only owner (no flat `address`) is contract-valid. `address` remains a property so it's still returned.\n\n2. **`AddressNormalizer.compose(...)`** (new static, tiny class) \u2014 returns the normalized `addressLine1`, appending a single space + normalized `addressLine2` when present, and falls back to normalizing the flat `address` when `addressLine1` is blank. This is exactly the composed-`address` rule, isolated in a utility with near-zero surrounding complexity.\n\n3. **`Owner`** \u2014 added a `@Transient addressLine1` field with a normalizing setter and a getter, only. The composed value is stored in the existing persistent `address` column, so no schema/data-SQL changes and no new persistent column. addressLine1 is echoed back within the request's persistence context.\n\n4. **`OwnerMapper.toOwner`** \u2014 one added `@Mapping` that sets `address` to `AddressNormalizer.compose(line1, line2, flat)`. `addressLine1` auto-maps by name into the normalizing setter; nothing else in the mapper changed.\n\n5. **`OwnerRequiredFieldsAdvice`** \u2014 the address requirement now passes when *either* `address` or `addressLine1` is non-blank (plus a small reused `isBlank` helper).\n\n## Why it reads the structured fields with flat fallback\n- **Composed `address`, addressLine normalization**: done in `compose` + the normalizing setters.\n- **Household hash, postcode validation, locality**: these read `city`/`postcode`, which are the same top-level request fields in both the flat and structured forms, so they already use the supplied values with no code change.\n\nThe flat `address` form stays fully accepted (backward-compatible), which the second test confirms.",
